  "strategy": "# Approach and rationale\n\nThis approach introduces a multi-resolution upscaling strategy to refine solutions iteratively across increasing discretization levels. Unlike the parent's fixed-resolution initialization, our method:\n\n1. Starts with a coarse resolution (64 points) for fast exploration\n2. Gradually upscales to 128 then 256 points\n3. At each stage, uses the best solution from the previous stage as a template to generate initial guesses\n4. Combines template-based initialization with random variations to balance exploration and exploitation\n5. Implements rigorous constraint handling and feasibility checks at each step\n\nThis approach addresses the parent's limitations in several ways:\n- Avoids being trapped in local optima by leveraging hierarchical structure\n- Efficiently explores the solution space at different scales\n- Maintains feasible solutions throughout the upscaling process\n- Builds on proven templates while introducing controlled randomness\n- Exploits the relationship between lower and higher resolution solutions\n\n# Mathematical specification\n\nWe represent h at resolution n_points = 64, then upscale to 128, then 256. Let:\n\n- h_coarse \u2208 \u211d^64 be the step function at the coarsest resolution\n- h_medium \u2208 \u211d^128, h_fine \u2208 \u211d^256 be upsampled versions\n\nAt each resolution level, the step function satisfies:\n1. 0 \u2264 h_i \u2264 1 for all i\n2. \u2211 h_i * dx = 1, where dx = 2/n_points\n\nUpscaling is done via linear interpolation, followed by random local perturbations constrained to preserve feasibility.\n\nThe objective at each resolution is:\n$$C_5 = \\max_k \\int h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx$$\nevaluated discretely.\n\n# Implementation procedure\n\n## Preprocessing and setup\n1. Set n_points = [64, 128, 256] as resolution levels\n2. Define dx for each level: dx_i = 2.0 / n_points[i]\n3. Initialize best_h = None, best_c5 = infinity\n4. Set time budget: 1100s total, allocate ~300s per resolution level\n\n## Coarse level optimization (n_points=64)\n1. Generate 20 initial guesses:\n   - 10 with structured blocks of varying sizes (2-32 points)\n   - 10 with completely random block patterns\n2. For each guess:\n   a. Run SLSQP optimization with 1000 iterations\n   b. Track best solution\n3. Upscale best solution via linear interpolation to medium resolution\n\n## Medium level optimization (n_points=128)\n1. Generate 10 initial guesses by:\n   - Taking the upscaled coarse solution as a base\n   - Adding random perturbations (\u00b125%) to block heights and positions\n   - Ensuring feasibility after perturbations\n2. For each guess:\n   a. Run SLSQP optimization with 1500 iterations\n   b. Track best solution\n3. Upscale best solution via linear interpolation to fine resolution\n\n## Fine level optimization (n_points=256)\n1. Generate 10 initial guesses by:\n   - Taking the upscaled medium solution as a base\n   - Adding random perturbations (\u00b115%) to block heights and positions\n   - Ensuring feasibility after perturbations\n2. For each guess:\n   a. Run SLSQP optimization with 2000 iterations\n   b. Track best solution\n3. Finalize best solution after feasibility checks\n\n## Constraint handling\n1. After optimization, enforce:\n   - Clip h values to [0, 1]\n   - Ensure \u2211h * dx = 1 via iterative adjustment\n2. If infeasible, revert to the best template-based guess at that level\n\n## Termination\n1. After finishing all 3 resolution levels or exhausting time budget, return the best candidate.\n\n# Search choices and useful variations\n\n1. Number of initial guesses per level: \n   - Coarse: 20, Medium: 10, Fine: 10\n   - Balance between exploration and computational load\n\n2. Perturbation levels:\n   - Coarse\u2192Medium: \u00b125%\n   - Medium\u2192Fine: \u00b115%\n   - Controls randomness while preserving solution structure\n\n3. Optimization iterations per guess:\n   - Coarse: 1000\n   - Medium: 1500\n   - Fine: 2000\n   - Increases with resolution to enable fine adjustments\n\n4. Upscaling method:\n   - Linear interpolation ensures smooth transitions between resolution levels\n   - Preserves the overall shape while allowing for finer detail\n\n# Compute budget and robustness\n\n- Coarse level: ~250s\n- Medium level: ~350s\n- Fine level: ~500s\n- Total: ~1100s\n\nRobustness measures:\n1. Track best solution at each level\n2. Revert to previous best if feasibility is compromised\n3. Handle optimization failures by resuming with next candidate\n4. Maintain fallback solutions at each resolution level\n\n# Validation and return contract\n\n1. Check feasibility:\n   - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n   - \u2211 h[i] * dx = 1 with tolerance \u22641e-6\n\n2. Validate C\u2085:\n   - Compute using np.correlate(h, 1 - h, mode='full') * dx\n   - Ensure no NaNs or infinities in evaluation\n\n3. Return:\n   - (h_values, c5_bound, n_points) tuple\n   - h_values is a np.ndarray of floats\n   - n_points = 256\n\nThe method ensures that all returned solutions are feasible and provides validation steps to maintain the integrity of the result.",
